(window.LADDER_CARDS["24a"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["24a"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"24a1.1","q":"What is a rate statement, what is a solution of one on an open interval I, and what is the statement's order?","a":"an equation tying an unknown function y of x to its slopes, like y′ = y or y″ = −y; a solution on I has the slopes the statement mentions at every x in I and makes the equation true at every x in I; the order is the highest slope the statement mentions","w":["an equation for an unknown number; a solution is a number that makes it true; the order is its degree","an equation tying y to its slopes; a solution makes it true at one x of I; the order is the number of solutions","a recipe for the slope of a known function; a solution is that slope; the order is the highest power of y"],"why":"The unknown is a whole function, and the equation is asked at every input."},
 {"u":"24a2.1","q":"What is a start for a first-order statement, and what are the two halves of every question about a statement and a start?","a":"a pair (x₀, y₀), and a solution with the start has y(x₀) = y₀; the two halves: is there a solution, and is there only one?","w":["a first guess y₀(x); the two halves: how fast and how far","a pair (x₀, y₀); the two halves: what is y′ at the start, and what is y″","the constant C; the two halves: is C positive, and is it whole"],"why":"A statement that answers both, for every start, fixes the future from the present. Showing there is only one is the hard half."},
 {"u":"24a3.1","q":"For y′ = ky with start (x₀, A), how many solutions are there on the whole line, and why does dividing any solution by the claimed one show there is no other?","a":"exactly one, y = Ae^(k(x − x₀)); for any solution z, the quotient g = z·e^(−k(x − x₀)) has slope 0, so it is constant, and its value at x₀ is A","w":["one for each C, y = Ce^(kx); dividing shows they differ by a factor","exactly one, y = A + k(x − x₀); dividing gives slope k","exactly one, y = Ae^(k(x − x₀)); dividing shows the quotient is 1 at x₀ only"],"why":"Zero slope means flat (14d): the argument covers every solution, not only those of a guessed form."},
 {"u":"24a4.1","q":"What is out of place in “y = Ce^(−x²/2) solves y′ = −xy, so every solution has that form”?","a":"checking that some functions solve the statement says nothing about the others; that needs a proof of 24a3's kind","w":["y = Ce^(−x²/2) does not solve the statement","the form is right only for C > 0","nothing: a family with one constant is every solution of a first-order statement"],"why":"“These solve it” and “only these solve it” are two claims."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["24b"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["24b"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"24b1.1","q":"How does a statement y′ = F(x, y) with its start read as an area, and what are the two gifts of that form?","a":"y solves it with y(x₀) = y₀ exactly when y(x) = y₀ + ∫ₓ₀ˣ F(t, y(t)) dt for every x in I; the form holds the start inside it, so statement and start are one equation, and it asks only that y be continuous: the slope is produced, not assumed","w":["y(x) = ∫ F(t, y(t)) dt; the form drops the start and needs y smooth","y(x) = y₀ + F(x, y)·(x − x₀); the form needs no continuity and no start","y′(x) = y₀ + ∫ F; the form gives the second slope and the start"],"why":"Area then slope gives back the function (16)."},
 {"u":"24b2.1","q":"What is the repeated integration for y′ = F(x, y), y(x₀) = y₀, and what did it give for y′ = y from (0, 1)?","a":"y₀(x) = y₀ and yₙ₊₁(x) = y₀ + ∫ₓ₀ˣ F(t, yₙ(t)) dt: feed the last guess back in; for y′ = y it gave 1, 1 + x, 1 + x + x²/2, …, the matches of eˣ","w":["yₙ₊₁ = yₙ + h·F(xₙ, yₙ); for y′ = y it gave (1 + x/n)ⁿ","yₙ₊₁(x) = ∫ F(t, yₙ₊₁(t)) dt; for y′ = y it gave eˣ at the first step","yₙ₊₁ = F(x, yₙ); for y′ = y it gave 1 at every step"],"why":"yₙ(x) = 1 + x + ⋯ + xⁿ/n!."},
 {"u":"24b3.1","q":"Why do the guesses for y′ = y settle everywhere at once on [−r, r], and how does the area form pass to the limit?","a":"neighbouring guesses differ by at most rⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1)!, whose sum settles, so by the at-once comparison technique they settle at once; then the areas of the guesses head to the area of the limit (23e1), and the limit satisfies the area form","w":["each guess is a polynomial, so the limit is one; the area form holds for polynomials","the guesses settle at each point, which is enough for areas to pass to the limit","the guesses climb under the ceiling eʳ; the climb technique passes the area form to the limit"],"why":"Settling at each point would not carry the areas."},
 {"u":"24b4.1","q":"What is out of place in “the guesses settle at each point, so the limit solves the statement”?","a":"the area must pass to the limit, and that needs settling everywhere at once (23e1)","w":["the guesses never settle at each point","the limit solves the statement only if it is a polynomial","nothing: a limit of solutions is a solution"],"why":"n²x(1 − x)ⁿ showed areas failing to follow a limit taken point by point."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["24c"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["24c"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"24c1.1","q":"What is ‖f − g‖ for continuous f, g on [u, v], why does it exist, and how does settling everywhere at once read in it?","a":"the largest value of |f(x) − g(x)| on [u, v]; it exists by 12c, since |f − g| is continuous on a closed interval; fₙ settles everywhere at once to f exactly when ‖fₙ − f‖ heads to 0","w":["the area between f and g; it exists because areas do; settling at once is the areas heading to 0","the largest value of |f(x) − g(x)|; it exists because f and g are bounded; settling at each point is ‖fₙ − f‖ → 0","the gap at the midpoint; it exists always; settling at once is the midpoint gap heading to 0"],"why":"It obeys the triangle technique, so it works as a distance between functions."},
 {"u":"24c2.1","q":"Continuous fₙ on [u, v] crowd in the largest gap. What follows, and where does the proof get one N for every x?","a":"they settle everywhere at once to a continuous f; each gap |fₘ(x) − fₙ(x)| is at most the largest, ‖fₘ − fₙ‖, so the N that answers ε for the largest gap answers it at every x together","w":["they settle at each point only; the proof takes a different N at each x","they settle at once to an f that may jump; the N comes from 12c","they have a thread that settles; the N comes from halving"],"why":"The line of reals has no holes, and neither does this space of functions."},
 {"u":"24c3.1","q":"A map T on D has ‖Tf − Tg‖ ≤ k‖f − g‖ with one k < 1. What follows, and how fast does the run from f₀ close in?","a":"exactly one f in D has Tf = f, and from any start fₙ₊₁ = Tfₙ settles everywhere at once to it; ‖fₙ − f‖ ≤ kⁿ‖f₁ − f₀‖/(1 − k): a factor k closer each round","w":["at least one f has Tf = f; the run closes in like 1/n","exactly one f has Tf = f; the run reaches it after finitely many rounds","exactly one f has Tf = f, but only starts near f lead to it"],"why":"It is 22h3 again, with the largest gap in place of the distance between numbers."},
 {"u":"24c4.1","q":"What is out of place in “‖Tf − Tg‖ < ‖f − g‖ for f ≠ g, so a fixed point exists”?","a":"the shrink must be by one fixed k < 1: on [1, ∞), x + 1/x pulls every pair closer and has no fixed point","w":["the shrink must be by k = ½ exactly","pulling closer gives two fixed points","nothing: a map that pulls closer has a fixed point"],"why":"Without a fixed k the steps need not add to a finite distance."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["24d"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["24d"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"24d1.1","q":"What does it mean for F(x, y) to be tame on a rectangle R, and how does a ceiling on the slope of F in y give it?","a":"F is continuous on R and one number L has |F(x, y) − F(x, z)| ≤ L|y − z| throughout R; if |∂F/∂y| ≤ L on R, the mean value technique in y, with x held still, gives the bound","w":["F is bounded on R; a ceiling on the slope in x gives it","F is continuous on R; nothing more is asked","F has |F(x, y) − F(x, z)| ≤ L for one L; a ceiling on F gives it"],"why":"Tame means heights that start close are sloped alike."},
 {"u":"24d2.1","q":"With F tame on R with constant L and |F| ≤ M, the step h is chosen with h ≤ α, hM ≤ β and hL < 1. What does each condition do?","a":"h ≤ α keeps x inside the rectangle; hM ≤ β keeps every guess inside it, so the map T of repeated integration sends D into D; hL < 1 makes T shrink with k = hL; so exactly one solution lives on [x₀ − h, x₀ + h]","w":["h ≤ α makes F continuous; hM ≤ β makes F tame; hL < 1 makes the solution smooth","h ≤ α keeps x in the rectangle; hM ≤ β makes T shrink; hL < 1 keeps the guesses in the rectangle","all three only make the arithmetic shorter; the solution lives on the whole line"],"why":"Fixed points of T are the solutions, by the area form."},
 {"u":"24d3.1","q":"How far can two solutions of one tame statement drift apart, what follows for equal starts, and why is the bound honest and grim?","a":"|y(x) − z(x)| ≤ |y(x₀) − z(x₀)|·e^(L|x − x₀|); the same start gives the same solution wherever both exist; honest because it holds, grim because starts a millionth apart may be a million times further apart after a stretch of 14/L","w":["by at most L|x − x₀|; equal starts may still part; grim because L is unknown","they never drift apart; equal starts give equal solutions; the bound is 0","|y − z| ≤ |y(x₀) − z(x₀)|·e^(−L|x − x₀|); they close in; the bound is cheerful"],"why":"A tame rate fixes the future, but small errors in the present grow."},
 {"u":"24d4.1","q":"What is out of place in “F continuous, so one solution”?","a":"continuity is enough for some solution, but not for one only: y′ = √|y| has many from (0, 0); one only needs tameness","w":["continuity gives no solution at all","continuity gives exactly two solutions","nothing: continuous statements have one solution through each start"],"why":"The shrinking map needed the constant L."});
